User: How does cross-contamination occur during food preparation and what surfaces are most implicated?
Assistant: Cross-contamination happens when we allow the invisible world of bacteria to travel from raw ingredients to clean surfaces and ready-to-eat foods. The kitchen becomes a web of potential pathways - your hands moving from raw chicken to the cabinet handle, knife blades carrying traces from meat to vegetables, dishcloths spreading contamination across every surface they touch. Cutting boards deserve special attention as the primary intersection point between raw and cooked. While both wood and plastic can harbor bacteria, maintaining separate boards for produce and proteins creates essential boundaries. Those knife grooves in well-used plastic boards become hidden reservoirs that regular washing might miss. Kitchen sponges are particularly troubling - their constant moisture and trapped food particles create ideal breeding grounds. Consider replacing them weekly or using dishcloths you can launder in hot water. The contamination zone around raw meat preparation extends further than most realize. When you unwrap chicken, invisible droplets can travel across your counter, contaminating the salt cellar, olive oil bottle, even the kitchen towel. This is why establishing a dedicated prep zone and cleaning methodology matters. Work from clean to contaminated, vegetables before proteins. Wash hands thoroughly between tasks - not just a quick rinse, but a full 20-second wash. Sanitize surfaces with a proper bleach solution, paying attention to handles, faucets, and any surface your hands touch during cooking.

