User: What determines the optimal contact time for different chemical disinfectants in water treatment?
Assistant: When it comes to water disinfection, contact time is absolutely crucial for ensuring every pathogen is thoroughly eliminated. Each disinfectant needs its specific window to work properly - chlorine typically requires 30 minutes of contact time in clean water, though I find it best to allowing a full hour when possible to ensure complete protection. The water's temperature makes a significant difference; cold water slows everything down, so winter treatments need extended contact periods. What many people don't realize is that the cleaner your water starts, the more effective your disinfection will be. Pre-filtering through quality systems removes organic matter that would otherwise consume your disinfectant, allowing it to focus on eliminating pathogens. For home treatment, I suggest using test strips to verify adequate residual after the contact period - you want to see at least 0.5 mg/L free chlorine remaining. Ozone works faster but dissipates quickly, while chloramine provides longer-lasting protection though it needs more time initially. The key is never rushing the process; proper disinfection takes the time it takes, and cutting corners here compromises everything downstream.

